---
import ManualLayout from '../../layouts/ManualLayout.astro';
import Crt from '../../components/Crt.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/paths';

const use = `$ ls /n/home
Desktop     Documents   Downloads   Pictures

$ cp /n/home/notes.txt .
$ wc -l notes.txt
      42 notes.txt`;

const remount = `$ /etc/nmount 10.0.2.2 9200 64 /n/macos`;
---

<ManualLayout
  slug="sharing-files"
  title="Sharing files with your Mac"
  description="Mounting a macOS folder inside Research Unix over Peter Weinberger's network file system, at /n/macos and /n/home."
>
  <h2>A folder from 1985's point of view</h2>
  <p>
    Choose a folder in <strong>Settings → Shared folder</strong> and it appears inside the
    emulated machine at <code>/n/macos</code>. Your home folder is offered separately at
    <code>/n/home</code>. Both are ordinary directories to the Unix side — you can
    <code>ls</code> them, <code>cat</code> from them, copy in and out, and compile from them.
  </p>

  <Crt text={use} caption="An Eighth Edition shell reading a folder on macOS." />

  <h2>What is actually happening</h2>
  <p>
    This is not a bridge invented for the app. Peter Weinberger wrote a network file system for
    Research Unix in the mid-1980s, to bind the lab's machines into one logical system, and its
    client has been compiled into every Eighth Edition kernel since. It has had nothing to talk
    to since Datakit was switched off.
  </p>
  <p>
    ipnx gives it a server again. The kernel's own <code>netfs</code> client does the mounting,
    speaking its original protocol, to a server running inside the app. The Unix side has no idea
    anything unusual is happening; as far as it is concerned another machine on the network is
    exporting a filesystem, which in 1985 it would have been.
  </p>

  <h2>Read-only unless you say otherwise</h2>
  <p>
    A share starts read-only. Turn on <strong>Allow the VAX to write</strong> for a folder and the
    emulated machine can create and modify files in it — which is genuinely useful and worth a
    moment's thought first, because the writer is a 1985 Unix with fourteen-character file names
    and no notion of your Mac's conventions.
  </p>

  <h2>Why your home folder is not the home directory</h2>
  <p>
    It would be the obvious design and it does not work. Eighth Edition file names are limited to
    fourteen characters, the filesystem is case-sensitive in a way macOS usually is not, and a
    great many names in a modern home folder simply cannot be represented. Making that the
    account's home directory would produce a machine that broke in a different place every day.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the account's home is on the emulated disk, where 1985 rules apply consistently, and your
    Mac's files are reachable next door at <code>/n/home</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Across a suspend</h2>
  <p>
    Shares are dropped before the app takes its snapshot and remounted after it resumes, and this
    is deliberate rather than incidental. The mount table lives in the emulated machine's memory
    and survives a snapshot perfectly; the network connection underneath it lives on your Mac and
    does not. Left alone, you would resume with a mount that could neither be used nor cleared.
  </p>
  <p>
    You should not have to think about this — it is automatic. If you ever need to mount by hand,
    the command is:
  </p>
  <Crt text={remount} caption="Port 9200 is /n/macos; 9201 is /n/home. The third number is the mount id." />

  <h2>Speed</h2>
  <p>
    Fast enough to build software from. The protocol costs a round trip per path component rather
    than per byte, so its performance is measured in <em>files per second</em>, not megabytes —
    about 265 small files a second as it stands. Copying one large file will feel slower than you
    expect and reading a thousand small ones faster.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the share is not there, check that the folder is still chosen in Settings and that
    networking is enabled — see <a href={href('/manual/networking')}>networking</a>.
  </p>
</ManualLayout>
